    "OtherAbstract": {
      "Type": "PIP",
      "Language": "eng",
      "AbstractText": "Preliminary cervical dilatation with Lamicel, followed by uterine exploration, was successful in removing 30 of 31 IUDs that had not been removable in an outpatient clinic. Lamicel is a polyvinyl alcohol sponge impregnated with magnesium sulfate and compressed to form a 3-mm-diameter firm cylindrical rod. The 31 patients in this study had been referred by a gynecologist or hospital family planning department with a diagnosis of \"lost loop requiring removal under anesthesia.\" The uterus was investigated with ultrasound to confirm the location of the device. If the device could not be removed conventionally through use of Bozeman's forceps and a plastic thread retriever, Lamicel was inserted and the device was withdrawn by traction on the sponge strings. Half of the patients in whom this procedure was successful reported it was painless and 28 were relieved to be able to avoid hospital admission and anesthesia. No patients experienced bleeding after the procedure that was greater than a normal menstrual period. At follow-up, all patients had healthy closed cervices. Pain on insertion of the sponge was directly related to the initial state of the cervix. If the cervix could accommodate a 3-mm device, then the passage of the sponge was relatively painless and dilatation was significantly greater than that achieved in cases where the cervix was tight. Parous patients and those in whom the tail of the IUD had retracted cranially experienced the least amount of pain. In general, Lamicel is a safe, effective method for dilating a nonpregnant cervix in preparation for uterine exploration."
    }
  },
